  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 344 PM CDT Sat Jul 11 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nAnomalously strong upper ridging is building into the Northern\nPlains this weekend into next week. This will introduce very hot\ntemperatures, while also promoting mostly dry conditions. High\nhumidity will also accompany this period. These conditions will\ndrive heat impacts for multiple days this weekend into next\nweek.\n\nThis upper ridge will stick around through the week, although\nthere is some differences in ensembles in how the crest of the\nridge may suppress some. Should this occur, this would offer\na chance for showers and thunderstorms, as well as some relief\nfrom very high heat as it is shunted further south. Details in\nthis potential scenario is uncertain due to mesoscale processes\nthat would augment the crest of the ridge. Should storms\ndevelop, it is reasonable to expect they would hold potential to\nbe severe given the antecedent high instability amid high heat \nand humidity as well as better chance for some increased winds \naloft within the crest of the ridge.\n\nOne aspect that may not have been previously message is\npotential for fire weather. While dew points and overall humidity\nwill be high, the very hot temperatures will allow for RH values\ninto the 30s. This combined with breezy southwest winds and very\nhot temperatures, fire weather conditions could ensue. Big\nquestion on fuel status, but general deficit of rainfall in\nplaces like northwest MN into the central and southern Red River\nValley may prime fuels for upcoming heat wave to be susceptible\nto rapid fire spread. A Fire Weather Watch has been issued in\nnorthwest MN as requested by Minnesota Interagency Fire Center\npartners.\n\n...Heat impacts this weekend into next week...\n\nAll guidance depicts very hot temperatures into the mid 90s\nwithin west-cental MN to mid 100s within the Red River Valley.\nThe locally hotter temperatures within the Valley is bolstered\nby a favorable downsloping-type wind direction out of the\nsouthwest. This will push heat indices well into mid to upper\n100s within the Valley, eastern ND, and northwest MN. Heat\nindices are also driven by seasonably average to high dew \npoints into the 60s and 70s. \n\nMostly sunny skies and generally lighter winds will push wet-\nbulb globe temperatures into the High to Extreme categories. \nAdditionally, Heat Risk will be at least Major across the entire\nregion Sunday and Monday, with some areas of Extreme \n(especially within the Valley). This is driven by abnormally \nhigh daytime high temperatures, as well as poor overnight \nrelief Sunday night into Monday morning with temperatures \nremaining in the mid to upper 70s coupled with high humidity.\n\nFor these reasons, an Extreme Heat Warning has been issued\nSunday through Monday.\n\nHeat impacts may extend into Tuesday / mid week in southeast ND\nand west-central MN. This will depend on how far an eventual dry\ncold front moves through late Monday into Tuesday. It is\npossible, additionally heat impacts (and subsequent headlines)\nmay linger in these areas into mid week.",
